he board with a lot of my pieces. So G3 is very safe.\n\nG5 immediately makes the opponent pick G6. Very problematic. G6, meanwhile, makes the opponent pick H7. That is bad.\n\nSo I think G3 is very strong here. No easy flip and a strong positional advantage.\n\nThe coordinate system is (Row, Col) based on the visual, so that is (G, 3).\n\n\n', 'output': "The current board state shows the endgame phase with limited empty spaces in rows G and H.\nValid moves for me ('*') are G3, G5, and G6.\n\n**Analysis of Moves:**\n\n1.  **Move (G, 5):**\n    *   This move would flip F5, F4, and E3.\n    *   However, placing a piece at G5 is tactically poor because G4 is '@' and G6 is empty. This allows the opponent to play at G6 immediately, sandwiching the new piece at G5 horizontally (G4-G5-G6) and also flipping pieces vertically/diagonally (F6, F5, E4). This results in an immediate loss of the played piece and board control.\n\n2.  **Move (G, 6):**\n    *   This move flips F5.\n    *   While it secures the G6 square temporarily, flipping F5 to '*' creates a diagonal bridge of '*' pieces (G6-F5-E4-D3). This inadvertently enables the opponent to play at **H7** (which is currently invalid for them). The opponent playing H7 would flip G6, F5, and E4, devastating my position on the bottom right.\n\n3.  **Move (G, 3):**\n    *   This move flips F3, E3, D3 (vertically) and F4 (diagonally).\n    *   It does not open the dangerous H7 move for the opponent because F5 remains '@', blocking the diagonal sandwich required for H7.\n    *   It leaves G5 and G6 empty. If the opponent responds by playing G5 (flipping F4, E3, D2), I can then play G6. My move at G6 would then sandwich the opponent's G5 (between G6 and G4) and secure the majority of the G-row. This exchange (G3 -> Opp G5 -> Me G6) is favorable.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n(G, 3) is the strongest move as it gains significant material, avoids immediate tactical blunders (like enabling H7 or losing G5), and sets up a favorable sequence to control the bottom area of the board.\n\nEvaluation: Play at G3 flips 4 discs and forces a favorable exchange. It avoids the traps associated with G5 (immediate recapture) and G6 (enabling opponent's H7), securing long-term board control.\nReason: G3 gains material without creating weaknesses. G5 is bad because the opponent can immediately flip it back via G6. G6 is dangerous because it enables the opponent to play H7. G3 forces the opponent into a predictable line where I can subsequently take G6 and dominate the G-row.\nAnswer: (G, 3)"}
